## Goal
Create a PCA of the six bulk RNA-seq samples from the gene counts and show whether treated and untreated samples separate in the first two principal components. Color samples by condition using the project design: A1, A2, A3 are untreated; B1, B2, B3 are treated. This is an exploratory visualization, not a statistical test.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |

## Method
1. In the `gars-bio` environment, run a Python script on the configured batch executor. Read the resolved tab-separated gene-count matrix without modifying it; use its gene identifier column and the six sample columns A1–A3 and B1–B3.
2. For each gene, calculate counts per million as `count / sample_total * 1,000,000`, then apply `log2(CPM + 1)`. Exclude genes with zero variance across the six samples. Center and scale each remaining gene across samples and run scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` with `random_state=0` on the samples-by-genes matrix.
3. Write the first two component scores and explained-variance ratios to a CSV. Produce a labeled scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2, with one point per sample, point labels from sample IDs, and color distinguishing treated from untreated. Include axis labels with explained variance percentages and a legend. Save both outputs under `results/`.

